#N canvas 0 0 700 400 10;
#X obj 20 20 inlet;
#X text 110 20 grid.pd: LED display translator. takes a logical "x y level" instruction on the inlet (level 0-15 \, this port targets a greyscale/varibright grid) and outputs it unchanged \, ready to feed straight into serialosc.pd's LED-control inlet (inlet 1). deliberately protocol-agnostic -- knows nothing about OSC or serialosc.pd's internals \, and does not decide WHAT a key press should mean (echo \, sample trigger \, pattern step \, etc -- that decision belongs to whatever calls this).;
#X obj 20 100 outlet;
#X text 110 100 turn 6 (2026-09-17): deliberately a pass-through for now \, confirmed correct interface shape by first building the echo behavior directly in tests/echo_test.pd against real hardware (see handoff log) rather than guessing at this file's shape in a vacuum. explicitly deferred \, not forgotten: (1) internal per-cell state tracking to skip redundant sends when a value hasn't changed -- Pd vanilla has no simple single-index table poke/read pair confirmed yet \, would need verifying before relying on it. (2) batch messages (/level/all \, /level/row \, /level/col \, /level/map) for updating many cells in one OSC packet instead of one send per cell. (3) bounds checking against actual grid dimensions (hardcoded assumption of 8x8 everywhere in this port so far \, matching the one physical device available to test against).;
#X connect 0 0 2 0;
